cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Value-Field Theory (VFT)"
message: "If you use this theory or repository, please cite this repository."
authors:
  - family-names: "Nuno"
    given-names: "T."
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
abstract: >
  Value-Field Theory (VFT) distinguishes a physical or real-resource state K
  from actor-indexed book-value capital positions K_i represented on balance
  sheets in monetary units. K_i is not a subset or partition of K; it is an
  actor-indexed monetary representation produced through ownership or
  attribution, recognition, valuation, and bookkeeping. Subjective evaluation
  and expectation states P_i condition actor-side processes and events A_i.
  Realized Delta K refers to resource change on the physical side and is kept
  distinct from accounting entries. Period physical and economic flows are
  mapped into monetary units and represented on profit-and-loss accounts; those
  monetary period amounts connect through closing, attribution, distribution,
  and other accounting rules to balance-sheet capital positions K_i. Surplus is
  therefore treated as an accounting-derived period increment or residual within
  a specified accounting and consolidation boundary, rather than as a physical
  primitive. Financial assets such as deposits, bonds, and receivables can be
  represented as recognized and valued balance-sheet positions K_i without
  treating their underlying contracts or legal relations themselves as physical
  K. VFT adopts resource-realization, activity-flow, and P-downside as
  constitutive rationality assumptions; empirically testable hypotheses arise
  only after projection-specific objectives, priorities, constraints, thresholds,
  or horizons are specified. Marxian categories such as use-value, labor,
  exchange-value, surplus, and accumulation can be represented on VFT, while
  Marx-specific value theory requires projection-specific conditions such as
  socially necessary labor time. Micro and macro descriptions connect the same
  physical K to monetary P/L and B/S representations through explicit
  measurement, ownership, valuation, and consolidation rules.
